WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:01.835
In Bakhmut Ukraine.

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:02.836
A brutal standoff

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:05.038
continues as Russian and Ukrainian forces

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.706
battle for control of that area.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:09.142
The head of the Wagner group, the Russian

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:11.144
private military company, admits

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:12.078
that Ukrainian forces

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:14.647
are fighting fiercely for every meter.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:16.783
A commander for the Ukrainian army

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:17.917
says logistical routes

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:18.852
in and out of boxwood

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:19.819
are still functioning,

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.221
meaning the supply of ammunition

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.256
remains open for defending forces.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.691
Joining us now

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:26.659
with more on all of this is retired U.S.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.661
Army Colonel Eugene Vindman.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.430
Brother of someone you also recognize,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.765
Alexander Vindman.

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:33.033
There they are right there.

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:33.800
But Eugene

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:34.434
Vindman was born

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:36.102
in Ukraine as the twin brother,

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:37.771
as I said, of Alexander Vindman,

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.305
whose central testimony

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:40.740
was part of Donald Trump's

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.808
first impeachment trial.

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:43.276
Eugene is now investigating

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:44.344
war crimes in Ukraine

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:46.379
as part of the Atrocity Crimes

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.448
Advisory Group created by the US,

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:52.018
UK and EU And Colonel, great to see you.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.553
I know you were just in Ukraine

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:54.954
for the United for Justice

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:56.056
Conference in Lviv.

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:59.025
How clear is the evidence of war crimes?

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.894
How is that picture coming together?

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:02.328
I assume it is coming together

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:03.563
because we've seen

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:04.831
I mean, I've seen so much evidence

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:07.367
just covering it on this show.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:10.170
Well, Jim, there's enormous

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.237
evidence of war crimes.

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:14.374
And we've seen evidence in Bukhara

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.409
in hostile areas surrounding

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:18.244
Kiev.

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.180
But we haven't seen.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.614
And what we've seen so far

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.116
is just the tip of the iceberg

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:24.284
is those areas

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:25.885
that are under Russian occupation.

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:27.220
So Mariupol

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.189
is probably an order of magnitude worse

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:30.457
than what we've seen so far,

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:31.391
an entire city

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:34.160
of a population of 500,000

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.329
that has practically been leveled.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:37.864
And we won't get a sense

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.899
of what
the war crimes in that area look like

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:41.601
until we get access to it.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.336
And what we're looking at

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.572
there is indiscriminate attack

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:47.907
on civilian objects civilians.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.610
It's a violation of a major

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.712
or critical principle of war

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.848
distinction where you cannot attack

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.983
civilians and civilian objects

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.852
And our group is helping

00:01:58.852 --> 00:01:59.352
the Ukrainian

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:00.520
government investigate that.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:02.555
And you've said Russian

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.457
leaders will never escape accountability

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:05.158
for their crimes

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:07.594
no matter how long it takes.

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:08.828
You mean Putin

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.731
when you say that as well, don't you?

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.166
I mean Putin.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:16.236
I mean his immediate lieutenants and

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:17.937
I mean the officers military

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:20.106
that are fighting this conflict.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.641
If we think about war

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:22.742
crimes, investigations,

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.577
they're long, they're painstaking.

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:25.612
The Ukrainians currently

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:28.715
have something like 74,000 open cases

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:31.618
and they don't have anywhere near 74,000

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:32.418
prosecutors.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.254
So it's going to require

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:35.522
a great deal of

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:36.489
time and effort

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.290
and assistance

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.325
by international organizations

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.693
like mine and others

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.563
that will come in and help the Ukrainians

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.432
with this enormous Herculean task.

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.334
And it's a process

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.636
that will take quite a long time.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:53.506
If you think about the way more to

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.108
the Germans,

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:57.510
we're still prosecuting cases

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.612
from World War Two last year

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:00.713
and Yugoslavia

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.582
which is a few decades old now.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:03.750
There are cases ongoing.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.119
And so it's a case

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:08.221
it's a matter of documenting

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:10.256
and then being prepared to

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.024
try these cases

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.059
when the opportunity presents itself.

00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:13.760
And last week,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.094
we saw one of Moscow's

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:15.828
biggest aerial

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.330
assaults in months

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.164
left nearly

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:19.666
half a million people without power.

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:21.100
And Kharkiv.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.835
How long can Ukraine hang on?

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:24.103
You've been there on the ground

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.239
several times. Many times.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.341
What is your sense of it on the ground?

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:30.210
Is there will to fight

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:30.810
and their will

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.477
to hold back

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:32.078
the Russians

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:32.812
just so strong

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:34.647
that they will bear anything?

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:35.448
Or is there

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:36.149
a time frame

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:37.650
that you're concerned about that

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.084
this is

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.118
this is about as long as the

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.320
Ukrainians can hang on for

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:43.890
Jim, I don't

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.425
see the

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:47.160
Ukrainians quitting any time soon.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.194
So, you know,

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:51.030
Russia's always had this fabled ability

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.667
to hold on and take a great deal of pain

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:57.670
from Germany

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.839
even further back in from the French.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:01.140
Well,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.142
the Ukrainians are from

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:05.011
that same neighborhood in World War Two.

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:05.578
They suffered

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.280
the same sort of casualties.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.983
And they have enormous reserves

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.452
and they're nowhere near quitting

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.820
and frankly,

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:14.454
they need,

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:16.456
with the help of the United States,

00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:18.691
with the help of Western partners,

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.026
they can bear this,

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:21.427
frankly, indefinitely.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:22.295
The air defenses

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:24.330
that are being donated, patriots,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.398
nay, Sams,

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:26.132
Irish teas,

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:26.799
these are things

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.968
they're going to help protect the skies

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.169
of the.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:32.739
The missiles that were fired typically.

00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:34.507
80% are shot down.

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:37.677
So 20% actually end up

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:38.711
reaching their target

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:41.314
so that the Ukrainians are doing

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:42.382
a magnificent job

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:43.683
of defending their airspace

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:46.152
and defending the ground territory

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:46.986
you talked about.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:49.322
But it's a grinding struggle.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:51.658
It's a grinding battle.

00:04:51.658 --> 00:04:52.859
Bloody on both sides.

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:55.261
But the Russians are suffering

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:56.929
enormously, much higher

00:04:58.231 --> 00:04:59.299
on their own.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.634
Well, Colonel, Eugene Vindman,

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.670
you're doing incredible work over there.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:04.737
Very important work.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.606
Thanks so much for your time.

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:07.874
Say hello to your brother for us

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:08.975
and make sure he knows

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.243
that we were able to tell you

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:12.512
you're both apart this evening.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.214
Thank you, Jim.

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:15.848
I appreciate it.

